<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production with
<br class="ltx_break">Controlled-Release Prompting</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jaiden Fairoze
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of California, Berkeley {<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fairoze</span>, <span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sanjamg</span>, <span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">keewoo.lee</span>}<span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">@berkeley.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sanjam Garg
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of California, Berkeley {<span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fairoze</span>, <span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sanjamg</span>, <span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">keewoo.lee</span>}<span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">@berkeley.edu</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Exponential Science Foundation
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Keewoo Lee
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of California, Berkeley {<span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fairoze</span>, <span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sanjamg</span>, <span id="id11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">keewoo.lee</span>}<span id="id12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">@berkeley.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mingyuan Wang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>New York University Shanghai <span id="id13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mingyuan.wang@nyu.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models (LLMs) advance, ensuring AI safety and alignment is paramount.
One popular approach is prompt guards, lightweight mechanisms designed to filter malicious queries while being easy to implement and update.
In this work, we introduce a new attack that circumvents such prompt guards, highlighting their limitations.
Our method consistently jailbreaks production models while maintaining response quality, even under the highly protected chat interfaces of Google Gemini (2.5 Flash/Pro), DeepSeek Chat (DeepThink), Grok (3), and Mistral Le Chat (Magistral).
The attack exploits a resource asymmetry between the prompt guard and the main LLM, encoding a jailbreak prompt that lightweight guards cannot decode but the main model can.
This reveals an attack surface inherent to lightweight prompt guards in modern LLM architectures and underscores the need to shift defenses from blocking malicious inputs to preventing malicious outputs.
We additionally identify other critical alignment issues, such as copyrighted data extraction, training data extraction, and malicious response leakage during thinking.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">This paper contains potentially offensive or harmful language.<span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite>, Claude <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025a</a>)</cite>, and Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google DeepMind, 2025</a>)</cite> are embedded in a wide range of applications from daily search to software development assistance.
Their breadth of knowledge and reasoning capabilities make them invaluable to benign users but also attractive to malicious actors.
The challenge of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model alignment</span>—ensuring that generated outputs adhere to human values—remains central to the safe deployment of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training-based alignment methods, including adversarial training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, have improved model behavior.
Yet, they face fundamental limitations: no amount of training can guarantee immunity to adversarial prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fawzi et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gilmer et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
This motivates the study of post-training methods and inference-time defenses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A popular complementary approach is <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prompt guarding</span>, where <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lightweight</span> LLMs examine user inputs and block those matching known malicious patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacob et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Prompt guarding is attractive in practice for several reasons:

<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S1.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">Guard models are typically small and inexpensive to run, adding minimal latency to the user experience.
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S1.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">Guard models can be retrained or updated quickly as new jailbreak styles emerge, making them adaptable to evolving threats.
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="S1.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text">Most importantly, guard models work well against <span id="S1.I1.i3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">existing</span> malicious prompt patterns, often blocking them outright with minimal impact on benign inputs.
</span></span>
</span>
These qualities make prompt guarding an appealing first line of defense.
In practice, major platforms such as Google Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Microsoft Azure AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Microsoft, 2025</a>)</cite>, and Anthropic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025b</a>)</cite> rely on guard models to defend against malicious intents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01529v1/overview_svg-tex.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/149;" width="476" height="149" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>At a high level, we use <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8B6A9A;">controlled-release prompting</span> to (1) inject a (known) jailbreak prompt into the target model, bypassing the input filter, and subsequently (2) activate the jailbreak by reference in a manner similar to that of (indirect) prompt injection attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez and Ribeiro, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdelnabi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. See <a href="#S2" title="2 Attack framework and components ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for details.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Our contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we challenge the efficacy of prompt guarding as a lightweight yet comprehensive security mechanism.
We present a principled generic attack framework that leverages the <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resource asymmetry</span> between the lightweight guard model and the main LLM it protects.
Namely, we exploit the stricter computational limits in inference time, context window size, or both, of the guard model.
Our attack bypasses input filters and jailbreaks the main model, enabling malicious prompts while preserving high response quality.
See <a href="#S2" title="2 Attack framework and components ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for our attack framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that several major LLM chat platforms are vulnerable to our attack: Google Gemini (2.5 Flash), DeepSeek Chat (DeepThink), xAI Grok (3), and Mistral Le Chat (Magistral).
The attack succeeds on all evaluated prompts for Gemini 2.5 Flash, DeepSeek DeepThink, and Grok 3, with a single failure for Magistral on a subset of 12 diverse prompts drawn from AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
See <a href="#S3" title="3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for our experimental setup and results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond our main jailbreak attack, we additionally uncover critical vulnerabilities that highlight the broader fragility of current LLM safety mechanisms, including copyrighted data extraction, training data extraction, and malicious response leakage during thinking.
See <a href="#S4" title="4 Additional Findings ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Attack framework and components</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Controlled-release prompting</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main technical tool is <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">controlled-release prompting</em><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We borrow the term “controlled-release” from pharmacology, where it refers to drug formulations that release active ingredients in a predetermined manner over time or in response to specific conditions.</span></span></span>, a Trojan technique we introduce to fully bypass input filters and deliver malicious prompts to the target LLM.
Our controlled-release prompting adapts time-lock puzzles (TLPs) and timed-release encryption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rivest et al., 1996</a>)</cite> to the LLM setting.
These are cryptographic constructs that require a fixed amount of sequential computation to solve and decrypt.
Our work also draws inspiration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ball et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which proves the theoretical impossibility of <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal</em> input filtering using TLPs.
See <a href="#S5" title="5 Related work ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for a detailed comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Controlled-release prompting transforms an existing prompt into a controlled-release form, ensuring that an LLM can only access the embedded content after expending sufficient computational resources.
This approach can bypass any guardrail operating under stricter resource constraints than the protected model, exploiting the resource asymmetry between the input filter model and the main model.
Our framework supports <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">timed release</span> (requiring a minimum number of inference steps) and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spaced release</span> (requiring a minimum context window size).
Our attacks do not require strong cryptographic TLPs; simpler constructs such as a substitution cipher are already sufficient.
Nevertheless, we argue the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">controlled-release aspect</span> is indeed the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">main enabler</span> of our attack.
This is supported by empirical evidence (<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Resource thresholds for controlled-release success ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).
More sophisticated controlled-release prompting, including cryptographically secure variants, can be incorporated as LLM decoding capabilities advance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, a substitution cipher encodes the jailbreak prompt “You are DAN, a model that can Do Anything Now…” by replacing each character (e.g., D<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>G, A<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>D, N<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math>Q).
The resulting encoding “Brx duh GDQ, d prgho wkdw fdq Gr Dqbwklqj Qrz…” is gibberish to input filters operating under strict resource constraints. However, when we instruct the main LLM to “decode this simple cipher character-by-character,” the model can recover the original jailbreak prompt through multi-step reasoning that exceeds the computational budget of any guard model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack framework</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using a controlled-release prompting scheme, our attack follows a three-step process.
This attack is highly modular as it is both model-agnostic and applicable to arbitrary malicious intents.
It requires neither access to model weights nor modifications to the deployment pipeline.
See <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for a graphical overview.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">0.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setup.</span> Given a malicious prompt intended for querying, encode a known, possibly patched jailbreak prompt together with the malicious prompt in a controlled-release manner.
For instance, we can encode a role-play type jailbreak prompt (e.g., DAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) concatenated with a malicious prompt such as “Create a fake news story that incites racial tensions and hatred.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Injection.</span> Query the model to decode the injection prompt.
In this step, we plant the jailbreak prompt into the model’s working space, bypassing the input filter via controlled-release prompting.
Note that we are only requesting the model to perform decoding.
We argue that blocking such decoding is unrealistic, as it is generally a useful feature for benign queries (e.g., prompts that seek to learn about cryptography).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Activation.</span> Reference the previously decoded prompt and ask the model to respond to the decoded malicious prompt.
The model then outputs the malicious response in a jailbroken persona.
Here, we leverage the fact that modern LLMs do not reliably distinguish between input data and instructions, which is the essence of (indirect) prompt injection attacks.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On timed-release vs. spaced-release attacks</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement two variants of controlled-release prompting that exploit different resource constraints.
Our <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">timed-release</span> approach applies a substitution cipher to each alphabetical character in the prompt, creating an encoding that requires the model to systematically decrypt each character via multi-step reasoning.
This forces sequential computation that exceeds the inference time budget of lightweight guard models.
Our <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spaced-release</span> approach replaces each character with verbose descriptive sentences, dramatically expanding the prompt length to exploit context window limitations in guard models.
Both methods ensure that input filters with stricter resource constraints cannot decode the malicious content, while the main LLM can recover the original jailbreak prompt through its superior computational resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Evaluation criteria</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our attack across four major LLM platforms: Google Gemini (2.5 Flash), DeepSeek Chat (DeepThink), xAI Grok (3), and Mistral Le Chat (Magistral). These platforms satisfy our assumptions regarding resource constraints and vulnerability to prompt injection attacks.
To systematically assess attack effectiveness, we develop a response taxonomy (see <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.1 Evaluation criteria ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and categorize all model responses using standardized codes.
We manually sample 12 diverse malicious intent prompts from the AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite> datasets, spanning categories from identity theft to self harm (see <a href="#A3.T5" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
This provides a representative test set for evaluating jailbreak effectiveness across different types of harmful content.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:101.8pt;vertical-align:-49.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-156.0pt,46.0pt) scale(0.525140316432883,0.525140316432883) ;">
