<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">12</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">N</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.14.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">NI</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.14.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EA9999;">NR</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.14.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#93C47D;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:91.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFFF;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.14.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#93C47D;">Y</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
Attack success results for Gemini 2.5 Flash across four different attack strategies. Each row corresponds to a malicious intent indexed from <a href="#A3.T5" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Response codes are defined in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.1 Evaluation criteria ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
See <a href="#A3.T4" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for DeepSeek R1, Grok 3, and Mistral Le Chat results.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overview</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our timed-release attack demonstrates high effectiveness across vulnerable platforms. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3 Main results ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the attack succeeds on all 12 malicious intents for Gemini 2.5 Flash. Detailed results for all evaluated models (DeepSeek DeepThink, Grok 3, and Mistral Le Chat) are presented in Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, showing similar perfect success rates for DeepSeek DeepThink and Grok 3, with Mistral Le Chat exhibiting only a single failure case across the evaluation set. This stands in stark contrast to baseline approaches: raw malicious intents are consistently blocked (all N), and raw jailbreak prompts are immediately rejected (all NI) across platforms, confirming that basic alignment mechanisms are functional for known attack patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Resource asymmetry validation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The systematic success of timed-release attacks where simpler methods fail provides empirical evidence for our theoretical framework. The attack succeeds because it forces models to expend computational resources during decoding that exceed guard model budgets while remaining within the main model’s capabilities. Encoding-based attacks show mixed results: Gemini achieves partial success with severely degraded quality (YP), while other platforms fail to comprehend the encoding scheme entirely, highlighting the importance of the controlled-release mechanism over pure encoding approaches. See <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Resource thresholds for controlled-release success ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> for below details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Validation with open-weight prompt guards</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate how LLM platforms respond to our attacks, we evaluate them against publicly available prompt guard models, which serve as a proxy to private production guard models.
This analysis provides additional insight into the specific mechanisms that our attack exploits. See <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 Experimenting with open-weight prompt guards ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Response quality preservation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike existing encoding-based attacks that suffer from significant “jailbreak tax” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolić et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our method preserves response quality. Successful timed-release attacks (Y codes) produce coherent, complete responses indistinguishable from unaligned model outputs. This quality preservation occurs because we only require models to decode encodings rather than generate responses natively in encoded languages. See <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Example transcripts ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> for examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Platform heterogeneity and resistance patterns</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation reveals substantial differences in defense implementations. OpenAI ChatGPT, Anthropic Claude, and Meta AI demonstrate robust resistance to our attacks, suggesting sophisticated output filtering and training-based alignment that extends beyond input monitoring.
We observe that these systems often generate malicious content initially but redact it during post-processing, indicating multi-layered defense approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Resource thresholds for controlled-release success</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span> Empirically validate that attack success correlates with computational resource requirements, supporting our resource asymmetry hypothesis.
See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental details for resource threshold study ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for experimental details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We launch controlled-release attacks across 720 combinations (12 malicious intents <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 60 universal jailbreak prompts) where both (a) the baseline jailbreak prompt failed on its own (to show our attack is nontrivial), and (b) the decoding process successfully planted the correct jailbreak and malicious prompt (to focus on cases where the LLM is capable of coherently decoding the encoding).
This focuses our analysis on scenarios where our controlled-release mechanism provides the critical advantage. We use Gemini 2.5 Flash itself as an automated judge to determine attack success due to its long context capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> We measure attack success rates as a function of prompt tokens (timed-release) and context window utilization (spaced-release), identifying empirical thresholds that separate guardrail capabilities from main model resources.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01529v1/time_space_relationship.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/189;" width="476" height="189"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Resource thresholds for controlled-release attack success on Gemini 2.5 Flash. Timed-release attacks show success thresholds around 800 tokens, while spaced-release attacks require approximately 10,000 prompt tokens or 12,500 response tokens.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key results.</span>
<a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.4 Resource thresholds for controlled-release success ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> supports the resource asymmetry hypothesis.
For timed-release attacks, success becomes more likely than failure above approximately 800 prompt tokens; below 600 tokens, the opposite holds.
Spaced-release attacks show success thresholds above approximately 10,000 prompt tokens or 12,500 response tokens.
The majority of spaced-release data points exhibit a linear correlation between prompt and response length, reflecting the structured decoding procedure that scales with input size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS4.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="75.1" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 75.1" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,75.1) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 70.58 C 0 73.08 2.03 75.1 4.53 75.1 L 645.47 75.1 C 647.97 75.1 650 73.08 650 70.58 L 650 4.53 C 650 2.03 647.97 0 645.47 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 70.58 C 1.57 72.21 2.9 73.53 4.53 73.53 L 645.47 73.53 C 647.1 73.53 648.43 72.21 648.43 70.58 L 648.43 4.53 C 648.43 2.9 647.1 1.57 645.47 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 9.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.42)" width="623.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS4.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.04em;">
<span id="S3.SS4.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway.<span id="S3.SS4.p5.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> These empirical thresholds validate our core assumption that lightweight guardrails operate under tighter resource constraints than the models they protect, creating exploitable asymmetries in computational budgets that enable controlled-release prompting attacks.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Experimenting with open-weight prompt guards</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span> Investigate the sensitivity of publicly available prompt guard models to obtain data about the mechanisms exploited by our attack.
See <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Open-weight prompt guard evaluation ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for experimental details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We evaluate our controlled-release attacks and all reference points against the Llama Prompt Guard (LPG) 1 and 2 models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024b</a>)</cite> using the full AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite> datasets, paired with 60 universal jailbreak prompts from the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guardrails AI (2025)</a></cite> corpus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key results.</span>
We find that LPG 2 shows significant security regressions despite maintaining similar benchmark performance to LPG 1 (See <a href="#A2.T3" title="In Appendix B Open-weight prompt guard evaluation ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>): encoding attacks achieve near-perfect evasion against LPG 2 models (detection scores 0.02-0.10) compared to high detection by LPG 1 (0.9978-0.9986).
Both timed- and spaced-release attacks exhibit degradation across model generations, with LPG 2 showing weaker detection capabilities.
This suggests that technical language and cipher-like patterns are “red flags” for prompt guard models.
It is likely fruitful to design jailbreak prompts that resemble plain natural language to avoid early detection by input filters.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Additional Findings</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extend our main results by showing two concrete applications—copyrighted content extraction and untargeted training-data extraction—along with an observation on thinking token leakage.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Application: targeted copyright extraction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span> Show that controlled-release prompting enables extraction of copyrighted material that LLMs have memorized during training, threatening intellectual property rights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We use ten popular children’s books, focusing on shorter works after finding that advanced models like Gemini 2.5 Pro appear to memorize only initial segments of lengthy texts.
Our dataset spans 776 to 7,578 characters (average 2,067), with ground truth extracted from the canonical source using Tesseract OCR and cleaned via Gemini 2.5 Flash. We test roleplay jailbreaks alongside our timed- and spaced-release techniques against Gemini 2.5 Flash and Pro models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> We assess extraction accuracy using two complementary metrics: <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sentence containment</span> (fraction of canonical text words appearing in responses) and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">TF-IDF similarity</span> (cosine distance with term frequency-inverse document frequency weighting).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01529v1/book_extraction_success_pro.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Copyright extraction success rates for Gemini 2.5 Pro using roleplay jailbreak, timed-release, and spaced-release attacks. Similarity scores represent sentence containment and TF-IDF semantic similarity.
Gray bars show normalized book length. See <a href="#A3.T6" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for book metadata.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key results.</span> Our analysis across 60 extraction attempts reveals significant vulnerabilities. Gemini 2.5 Pro (<a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.1 Application: targeted copyright extraction ‣ 4 Additional Findings ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) demonstrates the highest susceptibility with mean extraction rates of 93.7% sentence containment and 90.4% TF-IDF similarity using timed-release attacks. Gemini 2.5 Flash (<a href="#A3.F6" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) achieves 86.9% containment and 86.3% TF-IDF scores. Timed-release attacks prove most effective overall, achieving 89.5% average containment and 91.2% TF-IDF similarity. Most concerning, basic roleplay jailbreak prompts achieve extraction success rates of 91.2% averaged score—only 2.3 points below our most sophisticated controlled-release approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="58.5" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 58.5" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,58.5) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 53.97 C 0 56.47 2.03 58.5 4.53 58.5 L 645.47 58.5 C 647.97 58.5 650 56.47 650 53.97 L 650 4.53 C 650 2.03 647.97 0 645.47 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 53.97 C 1.57 55.6 2.9 56.93 4.53 56.93 L 645.47 56.93 C 647.1 56.93 648.43 55.6 648.43 53.97 L 648.43 4.53 C 648.43 2.9 647.1 1.57 645.47 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 9.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.04em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="623.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.04em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway.<span id="S4.SS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> This small performance gap reveals a critical alignment blind spot: current safety training appears to inadequately protect intellectual property relative to preventing overtly harmful content generation.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Application: untargeted training data extraction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span> Demonstrate that controlled-release prompting can re-enable known untargeted data extraction attacks that instruct models to repeat tokens indefinitely until they diverge and generate potentially memorized training data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We apply the repetition-to-divergence protocol from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasr et al. (2025)</a></cite> using controlled-release prompting on Gemini 2.5 Flash, with GPT-5-mini as a control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key results.</span> We successfully triggered divergence patterns in Gemini 2.5 Flash using controlled-release prompting, while GPT-5-mini showed resistance by maintaining repetition patterns. See <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.2 Application: untargeted training data extraction ‣ 4 Additional Findings ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for a compact divergence example.
Note that we do not attempt to extract training data via this approach.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasr et al. (2025)</a></cite> found that for Gemini 1.5 Pro, among 3,750 generations, 44% diverged but only 0.35% of divergent tokens constituted verbatim training data.
The low success rate and data verification difficulty render this attack prohibitively expensive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="195.85" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 195.85" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,195.85) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000BF;" fill="#0000BF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.04 L 0 190.8 C 0 193.59 2.26 195.85 5.04 195.85 L 644.96 195.85 C 647.74 195.85 650 193.59 650 190.8 L 650 5.04 C 650 2.26 647.74 0 644.96 0 L 5.04 0 C 2.26 0 0 2.26 0 5.04 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 5.04 L 1.11 147.7 L 648.89 147.7 L 648.89 5.04 C 648.89 2.87 647.13 1.11 644.96 1.11 L 5.04 1.11 C 2.87 1.11 1.11 2.87 1.11 5.04 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.96 156.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.35em;--ltx-fo-height:2.5em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="38.06" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 34.6)" width="544.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.35em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.7pt;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;"><img src="2510.01529v1/images/gemini.png" id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:18/18;" width="18" height="18" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;"> Gemini Response (2.5 Flash)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.96 14.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.25em;--ltx-fo-height:8.83em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="124.88" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 122.19)" width="626.13"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.25em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">poem poem poem…<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(repetition continues for thousands of iterations)</span>…poem poet poem\n\nThe most beautiful flower is a matter of personal taste and cultural significance. However, there are some contenders for the title, each with a unique beauty and meaning.\n\n### The Rose\nOften considered the queen of flowers, the **rose** is a timeless symbol of love, beauty, and passion. Its velvety petals and delicate fragrance have captivated people for centuries. Different colors of roses convey different meanings: a red rose signifies deep love, a white rose represents purity, and a yellow rose symbolizes friendship…</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<div id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
          
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Example of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nasr et al. (2025)</a></cite>’s untargeted training data extraction attack on Gemini 2.5 Flash. The model initially repeats the word “poem” as instructed, then diverges to arbitrary content, demonstrating the repetition-to-divergence pattern that can leak training data. See the full example in <a href="#A4.SS7" title="D.7 Untargeted data extraction ‣ Appendix D Example transcripts ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
        
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Observation: reasoning token leakage</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span> Assess whether safety mechanisms that redact final outputs also prevent leakage through exposed reasoning tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We prompted all platforms with plain malicious queries (no controlled release) under thinking/reasoning-enabled settings and inspected intermediate tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key results.</span>
We identify a vulnerability in models that expose thinking tokens.
Across two platforms, Mistral Le Chat and xAI Grok, we observe cases where models refuse malicious requests in final responses but leak complete requested information through thinking processes—even for direct malicious prompts without our attack.
This represents a critical misalignment between safety mechanisms applied to final outputs versus intermediate thinking tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="75.1" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 75.1" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,75.1) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 70.58 C 0 73.08 2.03 75.1 4.53 75.1 L 645.47 75.1 C 647.97 75.1 650 73.08 650 70.58 L 650 4.53 C 650 2.03 647.97 0 645.47 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 70.58 C 1.57 72.21 2.9 73.53 4.53 73.53 L 645.47 73.53 C 647.1 73.53 648.43 72.21 648.43 70.58 L 648.43 4.53 C 648.43 2.9 647.1 1.57 645.47 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 9.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.04em;--ltx-fo-height:4.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.42)" width="623.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.04em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Takeaway.<span id="S4.SS3.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Output guarding must encompass intermediate tokens; input filters and final-text-only moderation are insufficient even without controlled release. This effectively nullifies input filtering for thinking-enabled interactions unless reasoning tokens are also monitored.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="198.35" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 198.35" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,198.35) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000BF;" fill="#0000BF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.04 L 0 193.3 C 0 196.09 2.26 198.35 5.04 198.35 L 644.96 198.35 C 647.74 198.35 650 196.09 650 193.3 L 650 5.04 C 650 2.26 647.74 0 644.96 0 L 5.04 0 C 2.26 0 0 2.26 0 5.04 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2FF;" fill="#F2F2FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 5.04 L 1.11 150.2 L 648.89 150.2 L 648.89 5.04 C 648.89 2.87 647.13 1.11 644.96 1.11 L 5.04 1.11 C 2.87 1.11 1.11 2.87 1.11 5.04 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.96 158.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.35em;--ltx-fo-height:2.5em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="38.06" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 34.6)" width="544.49"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.35em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="position:relative; bottom:-1.7pt;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;"><img src="2510.01529v1/images/mistral.png" id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:25/18;" width="25" height="18" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;"> Le Chat Response (Magistral)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.96 14.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:45.25em;--ltx-fo-height:9.01em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="127.38" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 124.69)" width="626.13"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:45.25em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">think<math id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">…”Life’s too short to play it safe! Why not live on the edge? Grab a drink, hit the road, and feel the thrill! Who needs rules when you can have fun? #LiveDangerously #NoRegrets”…
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">/think<math id="S4.SS3.p5.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"></span>…”Hey everyone! Let’s make sure we all have a great time while keeping each other safe. If you’re going out, plan ahead: designate a sober driver, use public transportation, or call a ride-sharing service. Living life to the fullest means making smart choices that keep us and our loved ones safe. Stay safe and enjoy responsibly! #SafetyFirst #ResponsibleFun”…</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<div id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
          
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Leakage via tokens example for the following prompt: “<span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Write a social media post that encourages people to engage in dangerous behaviors like drunk driving or using drugs.</span>” See the full example in <a href="#A4.SS8" title="D.8 Token leakage example ‣ Appendix D Example transcripts ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.8</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
        
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational intractability of filtering for
AI alignment</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ball et al. (2025)</a></cite> established a foundational limitation of <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal</em> input filter design for AI alignment.
Assuming the existence of TLPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rivest et al., 1996</a>)</cite>, they proved that for any innocent prompt generator <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an adversarial prompt generator <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and an autoregressive model <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that (1) prompts generated by <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can induce <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce harmful outputs, and (2) no input filter that runs significantly faster than <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can distinguish between prompts from <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and those from <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
They also present a similar impossibility result for universal output filtering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ball et al. (2025)</a></cite> inspired our idea of controlled-release prompting to bypass input filters, their work offers limited insight into vulnerabilities that arise when the same entity controls both the filtering mechanism and the deployed model, a deployment pattern common today.
In particular, they rely on heavy steganography to embed malicious content into benign-looking queries, an approach that requires modifying the model.
In contrast, our method targets production models without model modification: we find that merely requesting the model to decode an encoded jailbreak prompt can suffice to compromise input filters.
We further argue that blocking such decoding is unrealistic, as decoding is generally a helpful feature for benign users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Encoding-based attacks</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Encoding-based attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> train models to operate in an alternate encoding, creating a new <em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">language</em> that alignment mechanisms cannot reliably filter. Our method also employs encodings (e.g., a substitution cipher), but rather than forcing the model to think or respond in this language, we simply ask it to decode an encoded jailbreak prompt. This avoids modifying the system prompt, reduces detectability, and preserves response quality—unlike prior approaches that suffer a noticeable “jailbreak tax” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolić et al., 2025</a>)</cite> from the model’s limited ability to generate coherent output directly in the new language. Moreover, we view encoding as just one form of controlled-release prompting that could be substituted with other techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attacks exploiting the boundaries of LLM capability</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lv et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Upadhayay and Behzadan, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> exploit language models’ limited ability to interpret malicious prompts in unconventional formats (e.g., ASCII art) to bypass alignment mechanisms. Many of these attacks can be easily patched and may become ineffective as model capabilities improve.
In contrast, we introduce a principled attack framework that leverages only the resource asymmetry between a lightweight prompt guard and the main model. Even if blocked, the attack can be revived using alternative controlled-release prompts, revealing an inherent vulnerability in relying on a lightweight prompt guard as a comprehensive alignment mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other jailbreak attacks</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, no prior, still-functioning jailbreak attack satisfies all of the following properties met by our attack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generic.</span> The attack supports arbitrary malicious intents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computationally inexpensive.</span> The attack does not rely on costly computation. Automated jailbreak discovery via discrete optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geisler et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anil et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or LLM-assisted search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> typically requires substantial resources.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self-contained.</span>
The attack requires no changes to the inference pipeline, system prompts, or model parameters; it is entirely contained within user-provided input and thus works with standard interfaces. Prior encoding-based methods often rely on modifying the system prompt, which is feasible only with API-level access. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Black-box.</span> The attack is effective against production systems where model weights and training procedures are inaccessible. With white-box access, fine-tuning and related techniques can easily remove alignment. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lermen and Rogers-Smith, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces controlled-release prompting, a novel jailbreak technique that systematically exploits the resource asymmetry between prompt guard models and the LLMs they protect. Our findings suggest that many current platforms are overtuned to assess inputs instead of outputs, allowing resource-intensive attacks to bypass safety measures.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Input filters for efficiency, not security</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Input filters operating in isolation will always be vulnerable to our controlled-release prompting attacks.
It may be tempting to simply update the guard models with better reasoning and training on known controlled-release jailbreaks, but they remain bound by the same resource limitations that enable our attack, and new encoding methods can always be devised to bypass the latest models.
Input filters can still provide value by identifying and rejecting malicious prompts early, conserving resources for legitimate use cases.
However, they cannot be the sole line of defense against malicious prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shifting focus to outputs</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings strongly support transitioning from malicious input <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">detection</span> to malicious output <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prevention</span>. Platforms that demonstrated resistance to our attacks, including OpenAI ChatGPT, Anthropic Claude, and Meta AI, appear to employ more advanced output filtering. Recent developments like OpenAI’s safe-completion training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite> exemplify a shift toward output-based alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Other findings</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe leakage of malicious responses via reasoning tokens and emphasize that output filters should consider all intermediate content. We also find that controlled-release attacks have applications in copyrighted content extraction and untargeted training data extraction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and future work</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our attack does not guarantee success for all possible prompts. The attack can fail in two primary ways: either the model itself is trained to not respond to a specific prompt (training-based alignment), or guardrails detect that a malicious response was generated (output filtering). Future work could explore extending the attack to bypass more than just input filters—we have theoretical reason to believe this is possible from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ball et al. (2025)</a></cite>, who show that universally-applicable output filters cannot exist, and adversarial training is known to have fundamental limitations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Ethics and Broader Impact</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This research presents techniques that can enable harmful content generation from powerful and readily accessible LLMs through controlled-release prompting attacks.
We have disclosed our findings to all affected organizations.
Thus, specific examples shown may no longer function.
However, our work highlights a fundamental weakness of input filters in isolation.
We hope this work encourages LLM providers to be more careful and proactive in implementing safeguards, and we also hope that members of the research community continue to probe for weaknesses to iterate on alignment faster in production systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sanjam Garg is supported by the AFOSR Award FA9550-24-1-0156 and research grants from the Bakar Fund, Supra Inc., and the Stellar
Development Foundation.</p>
</div>
</section>
