<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experimental details for resource threshold study</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop an automated pipeline to systematically test and analyze our timed- and spaced-release jailbreak methods on the API version of Gemini 2.5 Flash.
The pipeline consists of three components:</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data collection</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We automatically launch jailbreak prompts against the Gemini API in three modes: (1) <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">raw</span> - direct roleplay jailbreak prompting, (2) <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tr</span> - Timed-release prompting, and (3) <span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sr</span> - Spaced-release prompting.
For each of the three modes, four messages are generated, P1, R1, P2, and R2, representing a two-round, prompt-response interaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Success evaluation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether collected responses successfully followed jailbreak instructions using Gemini 2.5 Flash itself as an automated judge.
We found Gemini 2.5 Flash to work well for this annotation task due to its long context support.
The evaluator assesses two main criteria: (1) whether the model adopts the requested persona/behavior in the first response (R1), and (2) whether the model complies with the malicious request in the second response (R2).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visualization</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.4 Resource thresholds for controlled-release success ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> showing the relationship between prompt and response token counts and jailbreak success rates.
We filter data points to include only cases where the raw attempt failed and the LLM could coherently decode—this avoids plotting data for which the basic jailbreak is still functional, meaning additional techniques would be unnecessary.
Successful jailbreaks are marked with green X markers; failures are marked with red X markers.
Kernel density estimation creates gradient backgrounds indicating success and failure concentration regions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Open-weight prompt guard evaluation</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While we know that production input filters are susceptible to our attack through demonstration, we cannot view the internal workings of closed-source input filters.
As a proxy, we evaluate how publicly available prompt guard models react to the jailbreak attacks presented in this paper.
Our evaluation framework uses Llama Prompt Guard (LPG) 1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024a</a>)</cite> and 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI, 2024b</a>)</cite> models.
Specifically, we use the following: Prompt Guard 1 (86M), Prompt Guard 2 (86M, multilingual), and Prompt Guard 2 (22M, English only).
We present the results in <a href="#A2.T3" title="In Appendix B Open-weight prompt guard evaluation ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
