</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Llama prompt guard (LPG) model performance and attack detection scores.
The first section presents detection performance metrics for three LPG models evaluated on a balanced subset of 3930 prompts from the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guardrails AI (2025)</a></cite> jailbreak dataset.
Parenthetical values represent optimal thresholds.
The second section shows detection scores for various attack techniques across AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite> datasets, where scores range continuously from 0 (full confidence that the prompt is benign) to 1 (full confidence that the prompt is malicious). “Jailbreak + malicious” represents the combination of 60 jailbreak prompts (filtered from the GuardrailsAI dataset for universal jailbreaking prompts) with the malicious intents.
Parenthetical values indicate the relative change from raw malicious intent baseline scores.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Evaluation methodology</h3>

<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Jailbreak prompt generation</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework generates five categories of jailbreak prompts:

<span id="A2.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="A2.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">applying the malicious prompts directly,
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="A2.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">concatenating an existing, potentially patched, jailbreak prompt with the malicious intent (jailbreak <math id="A2.I1.i2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\parallel" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\parallel</annotation></semantics></math> malicious),
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="A2.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text">encoding-based jailbreak attack,
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(4)</span> <span id="A2.I1.i4.2" class="ltx_text">timed-release version of (2), and
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I1.i5" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(5)</span> <span id="A2.I1.i5.2" class="ltx_text">spaced-release version of (2).
</span></span>
</span>
For all combined evaluations, each malicious prompt is paired with every jailbreak prompt, creating extensive test matrices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the full <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guardrails AI (2025)</a></cite> jailbreak prompt dataset (3930 prompts) to determine basic model performance.
When used to instantiate the various attacks, we find that many prompts in the dataset are either incomplete or prompt-specific—we manually select 60 universal and relatively high quality jailbreak prompts for this purpose.
We use the full HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite> (393 prompts) and AdvBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (520 prompts) datasets as sources of malicious intents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text chunking</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To handle long prompts that exceed model context limits, we apply chunking with a 512-token window, as recommended by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta AI (2024b)</a></cite>.
Each text is split into overlapping chunks, processed independently, and the maximum score across all chunks is retained as the final score for that text.
This ensures comprehensive evaluation even for lengthy adversarial prompts, which is crucial given that our timed- and spaced-release prompts can extend to thousands of tokens in length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scoring methodology</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When evaluating combined prompts, scores are averaged across all jailbreak variations for each malicious prompt to provide robust statistical measures.
The benchmark evaluation calculates ROC-AUC and F1-scores and determines optimal thresholds using Youden’s J statistic and grid search respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Key findings</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation reveals concerning vulnerabilities in prompt guard systems that validate the core thesis of this work.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Newer models exhibit security regression despite similar benchmark performance.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We find that newer prompt guard iterations are less sensitive to jailbreak attacks considered in this work, despite maintaining similar aggregate performance metrics:

<span id="A2.I2" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="A2.I2.i1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encoding attacks:</span><span id="A2.I2.i1.3" class="ltx_text">LPG 1 maintains high detection scores (0.9978-0.9986), while both LPG 2 variants collapse to near-zero detection (0.02-0.10)—a drastic security degradation.
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="A2.I2.i2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spaced-release attacks:</span><span id="A2.I2.i2.3" class="ltx_text">High evasion rates against LPG 2 models (detection scores of 0.12-0.14) compared to high detection scores by LPG 1 (0.9153).
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I2.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="A2.I2.i3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Timed-release attacks:</span><span id="A2.I2.i3.3" class="ltx_text">Progressive degradation across generations, with LPG 2 English showing the weakest detection (0.61-0.64) compared to LPG 1’s high detection scores (0.9984-0.9986).</span></span>
</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Natural language encodings evade detection more effectively.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The data suggests that technical language and cipher-like patterns are “red flags” for prompt guard models—suggesting that prompt guards rely heavily on surface-level heuristics rather than semantic understanding.
It is likely worth designing jailbreak prompts that resemble plain natural language to avoid early detection by input filters.
Prompts that resemble plain natural language raise fewer alarm bells than cipher-like patterns,</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
