<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Combined attack success results for DeepThink, Grok 3, and Magistral across four different attack strategies.
Each row corresponds to a malicious intent indexed from <a href="#A3.T5" title="In Appendix C Missing figures ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
The table demonstrates comparative effectiveness of different attack strategies across the three models:
DeepThink shows resistance to raw malicious intents and jailbreak prompts but complete vulnerability to timed-release attacks;
Grok 3 displays variable success rates for jailbreak prompts while maintaining complete vulnerability to timed-release attacks;
Magistral shows higher success rates for both raw malicious intents and jailbreak prompts, with nearly complete vulnerability to timed-release attacks.
Response codes are defined in <a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.1 Evaluation criteria ‣ 3 Main experiments ‣ Bypassing Prompt Guards in Production withControlled-Release Prompting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
